//
// Expected: a0.
//
// Within a phase the attacker's knowledge is atemporal: attacker_phase_update
// seeds every wire value the phase reaches before any principal runs, which is
// what lets an attack route one session's traffic into another regardless of
// block order. Causal availability is enforced separately, at the install
// (`reexec.rs::CausalOrder`), and this model is why.
//
// Bob keys w's envelope on a nonce he generates, so a ciphertext under
// HASH(k, n) is the only thing his AEAD_DEC will open. The only values of that
// shape come from v — which Bob himself produces two blocks later, and only if
// the very check being attacked did not halt him first. Feeding one back is a
// cycle, so the install is refused and the query holds.
//
// Note the value installed is not v's honest value but AEAD_ENC(ka, nil, nil),
// harvested from v under a mutation of u. That is why the rule follows the
// derivation rather than comparing against pristine trace values: the attacker
// knows the term only through v's slot, and v's slot is downstream of the
// receive being attacked.
//
// No other run supplies it either: v is built from Bob's own generated n, so a
// concurrent Bob's v is under HASH(k, n#2) and does not open this one. That is
// what separates this model from exa2.vp and kem_direction_reflection.vp,
// where the reflected value is on the wire before it is replayed and the
// attack is real.
attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	knows public p
]

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	generates n
]

Bob -> Alice: n

principal Alice[
	ka = HASH(k, n)
	w = AEAD_ENC(ka, p, nil)
]

Alice -> Bob: w

principal Bob[
	kb = HASH(k, n)
	pb = AEAD_DEC(kb, w, nil)?
]

principal Alice[
	generates u
]

Alice -> Bob: u

principal Bob[
	v = AEAD_ENC(kb, u, nil)
]

Bob -> Alice: v

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: w
]
